1
00:00:00,207 --> 00:00:01,167
Hi everyone

2
00:00:01,357 --> 00:00:03,527
There's a concert coming up soon in Hangzhou

3
00:00:03,579 --> 00:00:04,970
Its discounted public-benefit tickets, face value 50 yuan

4
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:08,429
have been listed on the resale market at 988 each

5
00:00:08,809 --> 00:00:10,329
That's nearly 20 times the price

6
00:00:10,379 --> 00:00:12,019
The singer had been out of the spotlight for years

7
00:00:12,069 --> 00:00:13,679
but a variety show made her popular again

8
00:00:13,959 --> 00:00:16,509
It's a show marking 20 years since her debut

9
00:00:16,581 --> 00:00:19,371
No one expected ticket prices to multiply like this

10
00:00:19,422 --> 00:00:21,112
At the end of the last episode

11
00:00:21,162 --> 00:00:24,182
we talked about that Huaqiangbei phone, remember

12
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:27,818
1399 buys a phone with an original price of nearly 3000 yuan

13
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:30,752
Your first reaction: wow, I saved 1600

14
00:00:30,842 --> 00:00:34,132
But here, the 50-yuan ticket is listed at 988

15
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:36,822
Your first reaction: what a rip-off, nearly 20 times over

16
00:00:36,934 --> 00:00:38,304
One's a win, the other's a loss

17
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,700
But did you get the math wrong

18
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:44,010
How much you gain actually has little to do with the price tag

19
00:00:44,099 --> 00:00:46,349
Neither one should be calculated this way

20
00:00:46,434 --> 00:00:49,024
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics

21
00:00:49,074 --> 00:00:51,234
number 10, consumer surplus

22
00:00:51,484 --> 00:00:53,564
Last time we left you with a question

23
00:00:53,614 --> 00:00:56,524
That "I got a deal" feeling, how much did you actually gain

24
00:00:57,429 --> 00:01:00,400
A price tag shows an original price and a current price

25
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:03,223
Anyone can subtract one from the other

26
00:01:03,273 --> 00:01:06,543
And that's exactly the math sellers want you to do

27
00:01:06,943 --> 00:01:09,733
But who writes the original price? The seller, of course

28
00:01:10,043 --> 00:01:12,403
The government even set special rules to regulate it

29
00:01:12,495 --> 00:01:15,865
In economics, "getting a deal" uses a different number

30
00:01:16,275 --> 00:01:18,495
the most you'd really be willing to pay

31
00:01:18,635 --> 00:01:20,735
minus the money you actually spend

32
00:01:20,785 --> 00:01:23,805
and what's left over is called consumer surplus

33
00:01:23,881 --> 00:01:25,771
And that number exists only in your head

34
00:01:25,891 --> 00:01:28,281
it doesn't show up on the price tag

35
00:01:28,401 --> 00:01:30,070
and the seller really can't see it

36
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:32,621
Even economists find it hard to calculate

37
00:01:32,688 --> 00:01:34,198
If you really wanted to calculate it

38
00:01:34,248 --> 00:01:37,258
you'd have to find the number in everyone's head

39
00:01:37,314 --> 00:01:39,504
And there's an even finer breakdown of the concert's prices

40
00:01:39,894 --> 00:01:42,278
820-yuan tickets were being offered at 5000

41
00:01:42,328 --> 00:01:45,854
320-yuan tickets, two bundled together for 3600

42
00:01:46,114 --> 00:01:49,115
And the cheapest tier was actually marked up by the highest multiple

43
00:01:49,197 --> 00:01:51,087
One survey on live-show ticketing

44
00:01:51,137 --> 00:01:53,227
asked 1000 ticket buyers

45
00:01:53,547 --> 00:01:56,847
For shows they really love, over 80% of people would accept

46
00:01:56,897 --> 00:02:00,617
paying 10% to 80% above face value

47
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:02,627
One ticket, two prices

48
00:02:03,017 --> 00:02:07,037
Does the organizer's price really match the price in your head

49
00:02:07,135 --> 00:02:09,935
Whether you got a deal depends on the number in your head

50
00:02:09,985 --> 00:02:11,965
not on its actual price

51
00:02:12,024 --> 00:02:14,894
So what if something has no price at all

52
00:02:15,846 --> 00:02:18,766
If you work it out as original price minus current price

53
00:02:18,816 --> 00:02:21,356
there's one kind of thing where you'd think you gained nothing

54
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:24,426
yet you use it every day without spending a cent

55
00:02:24,786 --> 00:02:28,786
like the maps, search engines and email we use daily

56
00:02:29,026 --> 00:02:31,696
Their original price is zero, and so is their current price

57
00:02:31,756 --> 00:02:33,374
And in your head, there isn't really

58
00:02:33,374 --> 00:02:35,489
any expectation of what they're worth

59
00:02:35,539 --> 00:02:38,736
In 2019, in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences

60
00:02:38,736 --> 00:02:40,799
a paper ran a big experiment

61
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:43,249
They asked American internet users, how much would it take

62
00:02:43,249 --> 00:02:46,059
for you to go a year without a certain kind of free thing

63
00:02:46,409 --> 00:02:47,429
Let's start with the most extreme

64
00:02:47,589 --> 00:02:50,079
Giving up search engines alone for a whole year

65
00:02:50,144 --> 00:02:53,534
the median answer was 17,530 dollars

66
00:02:53,894 --> 00:02:56,764
For email, it was 8,414 dollars

67
00:02:57,034 --> 00:03:01,214
and for maps, around 3,600 dollars

68
00:03:01,287 --> 00:03:03,747
And you haven't paid a cent for any of these

69
00:03:03,897 --> 00:03:05,677
yet the price in your head is this high

70
00:03:05,727 --> 00:03:08,372
and that whole big gap is all yours

71
00:03:08,422 --> 00:03:11,837
The question was how much you'd have to be paid to give up

72
00:03:11,837 --> 00:03:13,269
this kind of free app

73
00:03:13,319 --> 00:03:15,419
not how much you'd be willing to pay

74
00:03:15,469 --> 00:03:17,809
And it was the same for the group with real payouts

75
00:03:17,909 --> 00:03:20,109
One in every 200 people was picked at random

76
00:03:20,163 --> 00:03:23,453
and whoever got picked had their choice carried out

77
00:03:23,544 --> 00:03:26,494
Whatever amount they chose, they really got that much

78
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:28,214
and they really had to stop using it

79
00:03:28,394 --> 00:03:30,467
For example, quitting Facebook for a month

80
00:03:30,517 --> 00:03:33,018
the median was around 40 dollars

81
00:03:33,068 --> 00:03:35,238
That's the price American internet users would accept

82
00:03:35,448 --> 00:03:38,538
And what about WeChat, navigation or search in China

83
00:03:38,818 --> 00:03:40,728
Nobody has really calculated that

84
00:03:41,118 --> 00:03:42,818
You can estimate it for yourself

85
00:03:43,228 --> 00:03:45,598
The way I see it, where the surplus is biggest

86
00:03:45,648 --> 00:03:47,908
is often where you don't feel you got a deal

87
00:03:48,218 --> 00:03:49,928
because there's no price to remind you

88
00:03:49,978 --> 00:03:51,918
how much you've actually saved there

89
00:03:51,968 --> 00:03:53,598
Back to the two stories from the start

90
00:03:53,818 --> 00:03:56,248
A phone with an original price of 3000 has you thinking

91
00:03:56,298 --> 00:03:58,778
I got it for 1500, I saved 1500

92
00:03:58,854 --> 00:04:01,694
while a face value of 50 has you fixated on the markup

93
00:04:01,744 --> 00:04:03,054
Whether it's a gain or a loss

94
00:04:03,364 --> 00:04:06,084
depends on how much you really need it

95
00:04:06,163 --> 00:04:09,113
And consumer surplus is hidden in that number in your head

96
00:04:09,247 --> 00:04:12,067
Before you pay, do you know what it is

97
00:04:12,982 --> 00:04:16,006
But consumer surplus doesn't mean the more you save

98
00:04:16,046 --> 00:04:17,092
the more you gain

99
00:04:17,442 --> 00:04:18,842
Saving the same 1000 yuan

100
00:04:18,892 --> 00:04:21,202
for someone short on cash and someone well-off

101
00:04:21,252 --> 00:04:22,622
doesn't carry the same weight

102
00:04:22,706 --> 00:04:24,726
And what you pay isn't just money

103
00:04:24,781 --> 00:04:27,671
That milk tea shop by West Lake, about 10 yuan a cup

104
00:04:27,721 --> 00:04:29,501
and the line takes over an hour

105
00:04:29,701 --> 00:04:32,861
The sandwich shop next door has a line 70 to 80 meters long

106
00:04:32,917 --> 00:04:35,127
Someone made a special trip from Wuxi

107
00:04:35,197 --> 00:04:37,817
and waited an hour in line for that treat

108
00:04:37,907 --> 00:04:40,927
That hour-plus is a price he paid too

109
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:42,510
So, before you pay

110
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:46,200
first form a rough expectation in your head

111
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,380
How much am I willing to pay for this

112
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:50,880
Decide first, then look at its price tag

113
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:53,900
Only if the price is lower is it a real deal

114
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:55,240
Double 11 just ended

115
00:04:55,290 --> 00:04:57,530
so the stuff in your cart is perfect for practice

116
00:04:57,623 --> 00:05:01,123
For each item, first ask yourself the most you'd really pay

117
00:05:01,503 --> 00:05:04,603
then compare that with what we actually pay

118
00:05:04,963 --> 00:05:07,543
If the current price is higher, it doesn't count as a deal

119
00:05:07,803 --> 00:05:10,433
This year, Tmall dropped its cross-store spend-and-save discounts

120
00:05:10,713 --> 00:05:13,558
and switched to a straight discount per item, up to 15% off

121
00:05:14,283 --> 00:05:17,415
The current price is shown upfront, so comparing is easier

122
00:05:17,465 --> 00:05:19,615
Back to that ticket and that phone

123
00:05:19,745 --> 00:05:21,845
The 50-yuan ticket going for 988

124
00:05:22,105 --> 00:05:24,145
if deep down you'd be willing to pay 1500

125
00:05:24,205 --> 00:05:27,085
then getting it at 988 is still a good deal

126
00:05:27,455 --> 00:05:29,335
And the 1399 phone

127
00:05:29,425 --> 00:05:31,385
for someone who came specifically to buy a phone

128
00:05:31,454 --> 00:05:32,594
might really be a good deal

129
00:05:32,874 --> 00:05:34,644
But whether it's really a deal or not

130
00:05:34,694 --> 00:05:36,984
depends on the price you have in your head

131
00:05:37,039 --> 00:05:39,369
And the ticket price is the same for everyone

132
00:05:39,608 --> 00:05:41,029
But what if the organizer knew

133
00:05:41,079 --> 00:05:43,169
deep down you'd be willing to pay 5000 yuan

134
00:05:43,228 --> 00:05:45,278
Next time, let's talk about

135
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:47,988
topic number 11, price discrimination
